Angela finds this to be a selfish choice.

1040
00:42:41.230 --> 00:42:43.210
Yes, he has diabetes.

1041
00:42:43.570 --> 00:42:43.890
Mm-hmm.

1042
00:42:44.350 --> 00:42:46.830
Creed wants to help Jimmy Carter build gnomes.

1043
00:42:47.030 --> 00:42:50.010
Does that just get tossed out there, and no one reacts to it?

1044
00:42:50.050 --> 00:42:50.650
No one reacted.

1045
00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:55.610
Toby has an idea, but he gets interrupted, and finally, Dwight announces he's not going

1046
00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:59.210
to participate due to the lack of evidence that charity works.

1047
00:42:59.930 --> 00:43:00.290
Hmm.

1048
00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:00.810
Yeah.

1049
00:43:01.630 --> 00:43:07.910
Nellie tells Dwight the participation is mandatory, so he says, fine, I picked the Global Relief

1050
00:43:07.910 --> 00:43:08.290
Foundation.

1051
00:43:09.110 --> 00:43:12.250
By the way, it was recently discovered to be a front for the Taliban.

1052
00:43:12.770 --> 00:43:14.770
And everyone's like, ah, Dwight.

1053
00:43:15.070 --> 00:43:15.490
I know.

1054
00:43:15.830 --> 00:43:19.670
I'd like to point out that Nellie has a beautiful bouquet of roses on her desk.

1055
00:43:19.910 --> 00:43:20.290
Oh.

1056
00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:21.370
I don't know why.

1057
00:43:21.490 --> 00:43:22.990
Do they compliment her fancy chair?

1058
00:43:23.170 --> 00:43:24.750
She doesn't have her fancy chair anymore.

1059
00:43:24.930 --> 00:43:25.670
Oh, no.

1060
00:43:25.690 --> 00:43:28.510
I guess she had to take it home, because Andy doesn't have it either.

1061
00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:29.050
Hmm.

1062
00:43:29.390 --> 00:43:30.010
I know.

1063
00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:32.770
Well, now we are going to go to Roy's wedding.

1064
00:43:33.010 --> 00:43:33.990
It looks lovely.

1065
00:43:34.170 --> 00:43:35.750
It's like a garden wedding outside.

1066
00:43:36.290 --> 00:43:38.110
Yes, I guess it's in his backyard.

1067
00:43:38.290 --> 00:43:40.170
Bob Vance and Phyllis are there.

1068
00:43:40.450 --> 00:43:41.830
Daryl and Val are there.

1069
00:43:42.250 --> 00:43:42.470
Yeah.

1070
00:43:42.710 --> 00:43:43.950
Roy's going to greet Daryl.

1071
00:43:44.110 --> 00:43:44.730
He's happy.

1072
00:43:44.830 --> 00:43:45.810
He looks great.

1073
00:43:46.090 --> 00:43:46.310
Yeah.

1074
00:43:46.550 --> 00:43:48.310
Super handsome David Denman.

1075
00:43:48.610 --> 00:43:48.990
Yeah.

1076
00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:51.590
There's a tray of mimosas going by.

1077
00:43:52.130 --> 00:43:54.190
And Jim and Pam are going to arrive.

1078
00:43:54.350 --> 00:43:55.630
Pam's still eating her banana.

1079
00:43:55.630 --> 00:43:59.530
They're walking up the driveway, and they're greeted by a waiter.

1080
00:43:59.750 --> 00:44:01.870
He's got mimosas on a silver tray.

1081
00:44:02.170 --> 00:44:05.350
This waiter has maybe my favorite line of the entire episode.

1082
00:44:05.350 --> 00:44:08.770
It's some real good, like, side-eye snark.

1083
00:44:08.870 --> 00:44:09.170
Mm.

1084
00:44:09.170 --> 00:44:12.050
He says, would you like me to take your peel?

1085
00:44:12.610 --> 00:44:12.830
Mm.

1086
00:44:13.290 --> 00:44:14.990
I just thought it was really funny.

1087
00:44:15.990 --> 00:44:19.030
We got a lot of mail about this opening scene.

1088
00:44:19.330 --> 00:44:21.070
Fan question from Allison W.

1089
00:44:21.210 --> 00:44:22.350
in Winnipeg, who said,

1090
00:44:22.350 --> 00:44:24.890
Do Jim and Pam live really close to Roy?

1091
00:44:25.110 --> 00:44:29.110
Why was Pam still eating her banana when they arrived to his house?

1092
00:44:29.510 --> 00:44:32.310
Or maybe Pam just savors eating a banana?

1093
00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:36.730
Oh, this is like some deep, deep leaning into banana time.

1094
00:44:36.870 --> 00:44:37.030
Mm-hmm.

1095
00:44:37.030 --> 00:44:38.430
Like, what is your banana clock?

1096
00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:41.390
Well, Allison, I'll tell you, in real life,

1097
00:44:41.610 --> 00:44:44.730
Pam and Jim's house is an entire day away from Roy's house.

1098
00:44:44.870 --> 00:44:45.050
Oh.

1099
00:44:45.050 --> 00:44:47.390
Because we shot these on different days.

1100
00:44:48.310 --> 00:44:50.730
And also, you know, when Pam is leaving her house,

1101
00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:52.770
she hasn't started eating her banana yet.

1102
00:44:53.050 --> 00:44:55.730
That was a choice I made because I knew I was going to have to

1103
00:44:55.730 --> 00:44:57.810
still be eating it when I arrived.

1104
00:44:57.890 --> 00:44:58.050
Yeah.

1105
00:44:58.190 --> 00:45:01.930
So I think we were thinking that she just waited to eat it in the car.

1106
00:45:02.090 --> 00:45:05.030
By the way, that's also why I gave myself the business of, like,

1107
00:45:05.090 --> 00:45:07.310
futzing with my earring as I walked out of the house.

1108
00:45:07.670 --> 00:45:10.670
I wanted you to believe, like, maybe I got in the car

1109
00:45:10.670 --> 00:45:12.490
and still futzing with myself.

1110
00:45:12.690 --> 00:45:13.710
I believed it.

1111
00:45:13.870 --> 00:45:14.050
Thank you.

1112
00:45:14.050 --> 00:45:14.730
You sold it.

1113
00:45:14.730 --> 00:45:15.290
Thank you.

1114
00:45:15.470 --> 00:45:18.110
I ate a banana on my drive-in this morning.

1115
00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:18.750
Yeah?

1116
00:45:18.970 --> 00:45:22.250
I have about a half-hour drive, and I only ate half my banana.

1117
00:45:22.490 --> 00:45:24.610
But you always only eat half your food.

1118
00:45:24.730 --> 00:45:25.210
That's true.

1119
00:45:25.430 --> 00:45:25.610
Yeah.

1120
00:45:25.710 --> 00:45:26.710
You fill up faSt.

1121
00:45:26.710 --> 00:45:27.130
I can't.

1122
00:45:27.130 --> 00:45:28.190
You have a tiny stomach.

1123
00:45:28.370 --> 00:45:31.150
I can't really be part of this banana time conversation.

1124
00:45:31.930 --> 00:45:36.150
Well, speaking of locations, Roy's house was filmed at a house

1125
00:45:36.150 --> 00:45:38.470
on Ventura Canyon in Sherman Oaks.

1126
00:45:38.850 --> 00:45:43.590
And Steve Burgess reminded me that we ran into a number of logistical problems

1127
00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:47.190
at this location, many based on the extreme heat.

1128
00:45:47.290 --> 00:45:48.890
Angela, do you have a temperature for me?

1129
00:45:49.070 --> 00:45:49.650
I do.

1130
00:45:49.830 --> 00:45:54.290
Per the call sheet, the day you filmed these scenes was Thursday, August 9th,

1131
00:45:54.370 --> 00:45:56.530
and the high was 103 degrees.

1132
00:45:56.530 --> 00:45:57.310
Yeah.

1133
00:45:57.670 --> 00:45:59.250
It was hot and it was sunny.

1134
00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:03.210
So we had to bring in a condor, not the bird.

1135
00:46:03.410 --> 00:46:04.390
Oh, the crane.

1136
00:46:04.550 --> 00:46:04.970
Mm-hmm.

1137
00:46:05.210 --> 00:46:06.650
To try to shade it?

1138
00:46:06.870 --> 00:46:07.310
Yeah.

1139
00:46:07.510 --> 00:46:12.610
We had to put up this huge, it's called a fly swatter in the biz.

1140
00:46:12.870 --> 00:46:13.010
Yeah.

1141
00:46:13.010 --> 00:46:14.250
A little biz lingo for you.

1142
00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:19.230
And it's just like a huge piece of sheery silk material.

1143
00:46:19.530 --> 00:46:24.990
They hang it really high over the location so that it lets in light and it's not like

1144
00:46:24.990 --> 00:46:27.430
too low on us, I guess.

1145
00:46:27.590 --> 00:46:31.610
But every time the sun would move, we would have to move the fly swatter.

1146
00:46:31.890 --> 00:46:35.290
So it would have to be like, oh my gosh, it's like a big thing to move.

1147
00:46:35.370 --> 00:46:36.450
It's an enormous crane.

1148
00:46:36.710 --> 00:46:36.850
Yeah.

1149
00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:40.410
We also had to move it from the front of the house to the back of the house.

1150
00:46:40.410 --> 00:46:42.930
And it was really time consuming.

1151
00:46:43.210 --> 00:46:45.090
And Steve Burgess sent me pictures of it.

1152
00:46:45.210 --> 00:46:46.890
So you can put them in our Instagram stories.

1153
00:46:47.130 --> 00:46:47.390
Oh, I will.

1154
00:46:47.830 --> 00:46:50.010
We also got a fan question from Marissa D.

1155
00:46:50.150 --> 00:46:52.170
in San Jose, California, who said,

1156
00:46:52.250 --> 00:46:54.270
Jenna, how many times did you have to eat that banana?

1157
00:46:54.270 --> 00:46:55.630
I hope only once.

1158
00:46:56.010 --> 00:46:57.430
Marissa, it wasn't only once.

1159
00:46:58.090 --> 00:46:58.870
How many times?

1160
00:46:59.070 --> 00:47:00.450
It was a lot of times.

1161
00:47:00.770 --> 00:47:01.710
That's all I remember.

1162
00:47:01.950 --> 00:47:06.250
When I watched the scene, I was like, I ate so much banana, but I was really committed to it.

1163
00:47:06.370 --> 00:47:07.790
But I also had a spit bucket.

1164
00:47:07.790 --> 00:47:12.150
So any banana that was left when the scene was over, I got to spit it out, you know?

1165
00:47:12.630 --> 00:47:16.170
So I probably ate half as much banana as it seems like I ate.

1166
00:47:16.290 --> 00:47:19.550
You're always thankful for a spit bucket when you're doing scenes like this.

1167
00:47:19.710 --> 00:47:24.290
I mean, remember how sick Rain got when he had to eat all the bananas because he didn't spit them out?

1168
00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:24.830
I know.

1169
00:47:24.990 --> 00:47:27.650
You know, bananas are a binding food.

1170
00:47:27.990 --> 00:47:29.330
They constipate you.

1171
00:47:29.330 --> 00:47:31.810
They will mess you up if you go too hard.

1172
00:47:32.070 --> 00:47:32.310
Yeah.

1173
00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:37.090
Yeah, you shouldn't eat that much banana, you know, in one sitting.

1174
00:47:37.890 --> 00:47:39.970
All right, next up, fan question from Caden M.

1175
00:47:40.130 --> 00:47:41.270
in Warrenville, Illinois.

1176
00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:46.690
Jenna, was it scripted that you put the other half of the banana on the plate, or was that an ad lib?

1177
00:47:47.310 --> 00:47:49.550
It's one of the funniest moments in the whole show.

1178
00:47:49.750 --> 00:47:50.570
I agree.

1179
00:47:50.930 --> 00:47:51.890
I agree.

1180
00:47:52.270 --> 00:47:54.190
When the waiter says, can I take your peel?

1181
00:47:54.190 --> 00:47:57.270
And she flops it on the tray and grabbed a mimosa.

1182
00:47:57.430 --> 00:47:59.110
I was like, oh, I like this Pam.

1183
00:47:59.330 --> 00:48:02.470
This Pam is ready to, like, take on this wedding.

1184
00:48:02.750 --> 00:48:05.590
Well, this was a scripted moment, and here's what it said.

1185
00:48:05.810 --> 00:48:12.930
It was scripted that Pam, quote, awkwardly stuffs the rest of the banana in her mouth and gives the peel to the caterer.

1186
00:48:13.490 --> 00:48:18.150
The caterer, by the way, was played by David Britz, and we had a good time.

1187
00:48:18.290 --> 00:48:19.070
He was great.

1188
00:48:19.570 --> 00:48:23.590
Well, during this banana business, Jim is going to get a phone call.

1189
00:48:23.590 --> 00:48:25.150
He's been getting lots of phone calls.

1190
00:48:25.710 --> 00:48:27.750
He's been shady about them, too.

1191
00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:38.650
He jokes to Pam that it's his ex-fiancee, and then he has a talking head where he shares that he has started a new business with his college friend, and Pam doesn't know.

1192
00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:48.770
Well, actually, he told Pam, but then they decided no, but then he decided yes anyway, so he thinks there's going to be another conversation coming.

1193
00:48:49.710 --> 00:48:50.810
Oh, big sigh.

1194
00:48:51.650 --> 00:48:51.970
Yeah.

1195
00:48:51.970 --> 00:49:03.650
So he's now just shared with the camera crew of this documentary, Pam's going to have to watch this someday, that he has gone behind her back and is keeping this secret,

1196
00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:06.650
and now a lot of people know but Pam.

1197
00:49:07.090 --> 00:49:07.450
Yeah.

1198
00:49:07.970 --> 00:49:11.530
Well, now we're going to see a scene where Jim approaches Roy.

1199
00:49:11.870 --> 00:49:12.130
Yeah.

1200
00:49:12.350 --> 00:49:14.230
I'm calling this scene snark alert.

1201
00:49:14.430 --> 00:49:20.910
Well, it's good that you did, lady, because I thought there was some snark, and I went to the shooting draft, and it says,

1202
00:49:20.910 --> 00:49:33.490
as Jim approaches Roy, in parentheses, a little snarky, oh, uh-huh, look, snarky, in the shooting draft, when Jim says, is it almost time to cut the pancake or what?

1203
00:49:33.790 --> 00:49:34.870
What a dick.

1204
00:49:35.030 --> 00:49:36.670
What a snarky thing to say.

1205
00:49:36.990 --> 00:49:38.950
Who is Jim's season nine?

1206
00:49:39.110 --> 00:49:40.130
Who is this guy?

1207
00:49:40.710 --> 00:49:43.290
Ugh, Roy couldn't be lovelier.

1208
00:49:43.290 --> 00:49:44.330
He's so gracious.

1209
00:49:44.330 --> 00:49:45.210
He said, you know what?

1210
00:49:45.250 --> 00:49:47.350
We appreciate everyone coming out so early.

1211
00:49:47.550 --> 00:49:48.770
It's just been so nice.

1212
00:49:48.950 --> 00:49:49.310
Mm-hmm.

1213
00:49:49.710 --> 00:49:53.830
And also, listen, he wouldn't have met Laura if it hadn't been for Jim, right?

1214
00:49:54.010 --> 00:49:54.210
Yeah.

1215
00:49:54.210 --> 00:49:56.170
And then Roy does get a dig in.

1216
00:49:56.290 --> 00:49:58.090
He says, really dodged a bullet on that one.

1217
00:49:58.210 --> 00:49:58.690
Am I right?

1218
00:49:58.950 --> 00:49:59.630
Ha-ha-ha.

1219
00:49:59.950 --> 00:50:00.110
Yeah.

1220
00:50:00.570 --> 00:50:06.970
But then, all of a sudden, we're going to find out that Roy has a $50,000 car.

1221
00:50:07.190 --> 00:50:08.050
Well, wait.

1222
00:50:08.210 --> 00:50:12.330
He started a gravel company, and it just took off.

1223
00:50:12.610 --> 00:50:20.230
And then Roy's brother, Kenny, walks up and says to Roy, hey, bro, stop wasting time with this haircut, and points to Jim.

1224
00:50:20.410 --> 00:50:23.770
And that's when he shares that he has a $50,000 sports car.

1225
00:50:23.770 --> 00:50:24.330
I know.

1226
00:50:24.590 --> 00:50:29.130
Want you to know, Kenny's scripted line was, stop wasting time with this limp dick.

1227
00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:31.230
Why?

1228
00:50:31.290 --> 00:50:32.350
That's making me laugh.

1229
00:50:33.050 --> 00:50:34.130
That's really funny.

1230
00:50:34.690 --> 00:50:37.190
We had a fan question, Angela, from Shelby A.

1231
00:50:37.390 --> 00:50:39.590
in Huntsville, Alabama, who said,

1232
00:50:39.730 --> 00:50:42.270
We see Roy and Jim interact at Roy's wedding.

1233
00:50:42.470 --> 00:50:45.030
Was there anything scripted between Pam and Roy?

1234
00:50:45.250 --> 00:50:48.570
I'm so curious what they would have said to each other after all those years.

1235
00:50:48.850 --> 00:50:51.110
Well, Shelby, I was really curious about this, too.

1236
00:50:51.250 --> 00:50:52.370
I went to the shooting draft.

1237
00:50:52.370 --> 00:50:57.650
There are no scenes with Roy and Pam, but there might be a reason why she never makes it to Roy,

1238
00:50:57.770 --> 00:51:00.110
because she gets some serious shade from his parents.

1239
00:51:00.390 --> 00:51:00.810
Mmm.

1240
00:51:01.250 --> 00:51:05.050
So in the shooting draft, it said, Pam stands awkwardly with two drinks.

1241
00:51:05.250 --> 00:51:09.410
She is clearly heading back to Jim when Roy's parents stop her.

1242
00:51:10.110 --> 00:51:11.750
And I think you've got to hear it.

1243
00:51:11.810 --> 00:51:12.790
It's in deleted scenes.

1244
00:51:12.790 --> 00:51:14.390
She gets some serious shade.

1245
00:51:15.210 --> 00:51:16.910
We just love Laura.

1246
00:51:17.170 --> 00:51:19.730
I couldn't imagine a better daughter-in-law.

1247
00:51:19.970 --> 00:51:21.810
It's great to see Roy so happy.

1248
00:51:22.090 --> 00:51:24.050
Oh, he's the happiest he's ever been.

1249
00:51:24.310 --> 00:51:25.090
How wonderful.

1250
00:51:25.870 --> 00:51:27.990
Laura's really easy to get along with.

1251
00:51:28.170 --> 00:51:31.870
And she's loyal, which is what I think Roy needs.

1252
00:51:33.070 --> 00:51:34.350
Nice to see you again, Pam.

1253
00:51:35.830 --> 00:51:36.570
Be well.

1254
00:51:37.810 --> 00:51:45.490
And so Pam is just left there standing awkwardly with two drinks, and she never makes it over to Jim and Roy.

1255
00:51:45.950 --> 00:51:49.450
So maybe that's why there's no scene between Pam and Roy.

1256
00:51:49.750 --> 00:51:51.990
Oh, I remember shooting that scene.

1257
00:51:52.390 --> 00:51:53.690
That was so fun.

1258
00:51:53.890 --> 00:51:54.870
I love the dad.

1259
00:51:54.870 --> 00:51:56.590
As he walks away, he just goes, be well.

1260
00:51:56.590 --> 00:51:57.210
Yeah.

1261
00:51:58.030 --> 00:52:03.190
So now you thought the biggest creeper, Dunder Mifflin, had left.

1262
00:52:03.530 --> 00:52:04.030
Yeah.

1263
00:52:04.190 --> 00:52:09.930
When we said goodbye to Robert California, we thought all of the perversion left with him.

1264
00:52:10.110 --> 00:52:12.390
But we were wrong, and oh, were we wrong.

1265
00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:15.430
Clark is just as bad as Robert California.

1266
00:52:16.010 --> 00:52:16.550
Yeah.

1267
00:52:16.870 --> 00:52:19.610
I mean, dare I say he's worse.

1268
00:52:19.830 --> 00:52:20.170
Yeah.

1269
00:52:20.690 --> 00:52:22.150
He's in the break room.

1270
00:52:22.150 --> 00:52:26.710
He and Pete are side-by-side, per usual, and they're having a snack.

1271
00:52:27.030 --> 00:52:29.430
Aaron is seated at a different table.

1272
00:52:29.830 --> 00:52:31.730
Clark is going to take a phone call.

1273
00:52:32.170 --> 00:52:37.610
Clearly, the person on the other line is asking if he knows anyone who could be a newscaster.

1274
00:52:37.790 --> 00:52:40.970
Because, you know, that's how you become a newscaster, everybody.

1275
00:52:41.210 --> 00:52:41.570
Yeah.

1276
00:52:41.790 --> 00:52:44.310
That's how it happens in the break room at the paper company.

1277
00:52:44.490 --> 00:52:46.470
It doesn't require a journalism degree.

1278
00:52:46.590 --> 00:52:47.190
No, no, no.

1279
00:52:47.410 --> 00:52:51.710
It just requires a creep making a fakey phone call to try to get a girl back to his house.

1280
00:52:51.710 --> 00:52:52.550
What a creep.

1281
00:52:53.010 --> 00:52:53.210
Yeah.

1282
00:52:53.570 --> 00:52:54.990
Pete doesn't like this.

1283
00:52:55.170 --> 00:52:55.450
No.

1284
00:52:55.630 --> 00:52:56.530
He has a frown.

1285
00:52:56.710 --> 00:52:59.930
He's also drinking out of a Hers potato chip mug.

1286
00:53:00.310 --> 00:53:00.730
Oh.

1287
00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:02.270
Little shout-out to Hers.

1288
00:53:02.610 --> 00:53:02.810
Yeah.

1289
00:53:03.190 --> 00:53:06.770
Yeah, Clark convinces Aaron to make this audition tape, right?

1290
00:53:06.930 --> 00:53:08.130
Yeah, come to his house.

1291
00:53:08.130 --> 00:53:13.730
Yeah, maybe have a few different wardrobe looks, some lipstick, some wine to loosen up.

1292
00:53:14.170 --> 00:53:14.390
Yeah.

1293
00:53:14.910 --> 00:53:16.790
Pete has a talking head where he says, you know what?

1294
00:53:16.830 --> 00:53:17.810
I'm not friends with Clark.

1295
00:53:17.990 --> 00:53:20.430
He's just the douche sitting next to me at the office.

1296
00:53:21.250 --> 00:53:21.650
Yeah.

1297
00:53:22.090 --> 00:53:25.910
Aaron says she never thought about being more than a receptioniSt.

1298
00:53:26.010 --> 00:53:27.610
That's just the ad that she answered.

1299
00:53:27.750 --> 00:53:29.870
What if she had answered an ad for another job?

1300
00:53:29.990 --> 00:53:30.810
Like a CEO.

1301
00:53:31.050 --> 00:53:31.990
Or a brain surgeon.

1302
00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:32.330
Yeah.

1303
00:53:32.450 --> 00:53:33.930
She could have been either of those.

1304
00:53:34.130 --> 00:53:34.450
Yeah.

1305
00:53:34.450 --> 00:53:41.850
Well, listen, this might be a good place to tell anybody who is an aspiring actor or on-air

1306
00:53:41.850 --> 00:53:45.510
personality that you should never go to an audition in someone's home.

1307
00:53:45.890 --> 00:53:47.270
It doesn't happen that way.

1308
00:53:47.470 --> 00:53:48.090
And you know what?

1309
00:53:48.090 --> 00:53:53.830
It seems obvious, but actually, when I first came to L.A., there were a lot of what looked

1310
00:53:53.830 --> 00:53:57.090
like legitimate auditions in, like, the L.A.

1311
00:53:57.550 --> 00:54:00.750
Weekly that happened to take place at people's

1312
00:54:00.750 --> 00:54:01.170
houses.

1313
00:54:01.630 --> 00:54:04.110
And I had to have someone tell me not to do that.

1314
00:54:04.110 --> 00:54:04.590
Yeah.

1315
00:54:05.050 --> 00:54:05.830
Oh, for sure.

1316
00:54:05.950 --> 00:54:10.810
I remember there was, like, a flyer, like, I'm making an independent movie, holding auditions.

1317
00:54:11.650 --> 00:54:14.730
But they were, like, at his garage, at his house, you know?

1318
00:54:14.850 --> 00:54:16.110
And you're like, oh, well, it's independent.

1319
00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:17.050
Maybe they're on a budget.

1320
00:54:17.610 --> 00:54:17.930
Right.

1321
00:54:17.930 --> 00:54:19.910
Maybe they're working out of their home for this project.

1322
00:54:20.270 --> 00:54:20.670
No.

1323
00:54:20.870 --> 00:54:21.330
Don't go.

1324
00:54:21.530 --> 00:54:21.870
I mean—

1325
00:54:21.870 --> 00:54:22.610
Don't go to anyone's house.

1326
00:54:22.810 --> 00:54:26.730
Listen, we already told the story of what happened when I showed up for the International

1327
00:54:26.730 --> 00:54:28.950
Spice Girls commercial in the guy's condo.

1328
00:54:29.590 --> 00:54:29.990
Right?

1329
00:54:30.130 --> 00:54:30.730
Yes, Jen.

1330
00:54:30.730 --> 00:54:31.070
Okay.

1331
00:54:31.410 --> 00:54:34.230
So, unfortunately, Erin doesn't have anyone cluing her in.

1332
00:54:34.230 --> 00:54:35.710
Well, we'll get to it.

1333
00:54:35.810 --> 00:54:36.650
Pete's going to try.

1334
00:54:36.850 --> 00:54:40.270
I have something from deleted scenes, but I'm going to share it later when it happens.

1335
00:54:40.510 --> 00:54:42.770
Why don't we take a break, and I'll look forward to hearing that.

1336
00:54:42.910 --> 00:54:43.110
Okay.

1337
00:54:54.390 --> 00:54:58.290
We are back, and Roy is going to toast his bride.

1338
00:54:58.650 --> 00:54:59.950
They're so happy.

1339
00:55:00.210 --> 00:55:00.730
Yes.

1340
00:55:00.970 --> 00:55:05.290
His wife, Laura, was played by Sarah Chase, and guess what?

1341
00:55:05.490 --> 00:55:06.990
He has a surprise for her.

1342
00:55:07.150 --> 00:55:07.750
He does.

1343
00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:13.210
You know, Laura thought he was taking boxing lessons, but guess what he was doing the whole

1344
00:55:13.210 --> 00:55:13.610
time?

1345
00:55:14.070 --> 00:55:19.050
Taking piano lessons so he could serenade her on their wedding day.

1346
00:55:19.270 --> 00:55:19.710
Yes.

1347
00:55:19.830 --> 00:55:22.830
He starts to sing She's Got Away by Billy Joel.

1348
00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:26.310
Pam and Jim do not know what to make of this.

1349
00:55:26.610 --> 00:55:27.730
Phyllis and Bob make out.

1350
00:55:27.730 --> 00:55:28.890
They make out hardcore.

1351
00:55:29.570 --> 00:55:33.090
It did say in the shooting draft that Daryl wipes a tear from his eye.

1352
00:55:33.670 --> 00:55:35.170
He's so happy for his buddy.

1353
00:55:35.830 --> 00:55:40.390
Per the call sheets, the piano scene was filmed on Friday, August 10th, and the high this

1354
00:55:40.390 --> 00:55:42.270
day was also 103.

1355
00:55:42.950 --> 00:55:45.230
Well, we got a fan question from Veronica L.

1356
00:55:45.370 --> 00:55:46.250
in Illinois, who said,

1357
00:55:46.330 --> 00:55:49.430
did David Denman play the piano in the wedding reception scene?

1358
00:55:49.770 --> 00:55:50.710
No, Veronica.

1359
00:55:50.790 --> 00:55:51.710
He's faking.

1360
00:55:52.470 --> 00:55:56.910
He played the first couple of notes when we're close up on his hands and we pull back, but

1361
00:55:56.910 --> 00:55:59.410
then you'll notice the camera never shows his hands again.

1362
00:55:59.610 --> 00:56:06.150
He was pretending, and an actual piano player by the name of Matthew Angst was playing another

1363
00:56:06.150 --> 00:56:09.350
piano off camera so that David could sing along.

1364
00:56:09.350 --> 00:56:13.690
And they met for a little piano rehearsal that morning at 7 in the morning, and that's when

1365
00:56:13.690 --> 00:56:16.290
they taught David how to play those opening notes.

1366
00:56:16.670 --> 00:56:19.430
And I was there for that, and it was very, very charming.

1367
00:56:19.710 --> 00:56:21.890
You know, I was just so excited to have David back.

1368
00:56:22.170 --> 00:56:26.730
He was one of my best friends from the early days of shooting, and we'd lost touch a little

1369
00:56:26.730 --> 00:56:28.790
bit after we weren't seeing each other all the time.

1370
00:56:28.790 --> 00:56:33.230
So I spent most of this wedding, ironically, with Roy.

1371
00:56:33.450 --> 00:56:33.790
With Roy.

1372
00:56:34.310 --> 00:56:35.010
As Pam.

1373
00:56:35.030 --> 00:56:36.730
Catching up with your friend David.

1374
00:56:36.910 --> 00:56:37.270
Completely.

1375
00:56:37.870 --> 00:56:41.490
We also had a really fun fan theory from Nikita C.

1376
00:56:41.730 --> 00:56:44.070
in Sydney, Australia, who said,

1377
00:56:44.190 --> 00:56:46.150
did Daryl teach Roy the piano?

1378
00:56:46.390 --> 00:56:50.730
Because as Roy sits down to play, Daryl is the one to call out and encourage him.

1379
00:56:50.790 --> 00:56:51.850
What do you ladies think?

1380
00:56:52.430 --> 00:56:53.950
Well, I love that idea.

1381
00:56:54.070 --> 00:56:54.650
Me too.

1382
00:56:55.010 --> 00:56:55.950
I do.

1383
00:56:55.950 --> 00:57:01.710
And also, in the shooting draft, it had how proud Daryl was, and he's tearing up.

1384
00:57:01.870 --> 00:57:06.050
Yeah, I like to believe that he was sneaking off to learn piano from Daryl.

1385
00:57:06.370 --> 00:57:11.210
Well, I think it's time for another get-to-know-our-cast-and-crew moment from the call sheet.

1386
00:57:11.690 --> 00:57:16.490
This is about our department head makeup artist, Laverne Carcuzzi-Milazzo.

1387
00:57:16.810 --> 00:57:18.550
Things you didn't know about Laverne.

1388
00:57:18.670 --> 00:57:19.990
Laverne speaks Italian.

1389
00:57:20.510 --> 00:57:23.510
The prettiest place she has ever been is the Maldives.

1390
00:57:23.510 --> 00:57:28.750
When she was in school, she wanted to be an archaeologist, and her first encounter with

1391
00:57:28.750 --> 00:57:32.150
the film and TV world was on a non-union Prince video.

1392
00:57:32.810 --> 00:57:36.070
Her favorite thing to do on the weekend is not set an alarm.

1393
00:57:36.610 --> 00:57:36.970
Ah.

1394
00:57:37.510 --> 00:57:38.970
I love these tidbits.

1395
00:57:38.970 --> 00:57:39.530
I do too.

1396
00:57:39.950 --> 00:57:44.270
I hope they're interesting to other people who don't know these people, but I'm like,

1397
00:57:44.330 --> 00:57:44.610
huh.

1398
00:57:44.970 --> 00:57:46.910
I love knowing Laverne a little better.

1399
00:57:46.910 --> 00:57:49.950
I also love that there's a pattern to the questions.

1400
00:57:49.970 --> 00:57:51.810
It's how many languages do you speak?

1401
00:57:52.370 --> 00:57:55.090
What's a place that you've been to that you loved?

1402
00:57:55.370 --> 00:57:59.170
And then also, when did you first come to the film and TV industry?

1403
00:57:59.490 --> 00:58:01.410
And then what do you like to do on the weekend?

1404
00:58:01.410 --> 00:58:03.390
I like this range of questions.

1405
00:58:03.650 --> 00:58:04.930
It's a really good set of questions.

1406
00:58:05.090 --> 00:58:07.790
Maybe we should be incorporating these into our interviews, people.

1407
00:58:08.210 --> 00:58:08.650
Yes.

1408
00:58:09.050 --> 00:58:10.410
Oh, noted.

1409
00:58:10.970 --> 00:58:11.950
I think we do it.

1410
00:58:11.950 --> 00:58:14.910
Jim and Pam are shook up by the wedding.

1411
00:58:15.470 --> 00:58:17.570
I mean, talk about mouth agape.

1412
00:58:17.850 --> 00:58:18.270
Yes.

1413
00:58:18.390 --> 00:58:21.910
When Roy starts playing the piano and singing, Pam is like, what?

1414
00:58:22.350 --> 00:58:26.410
The whole point of this storyline, and it's interesting because talk about digital clutter,

1415
00:58:26.710 --> 00:58:31.650
I put Roy's wedding into my email because I was looking for tidbits from Steve Burgess,

1416
00:58:31.790 --> 00:58:36.910
and I found these old emails between me, Greg, Alison Silverman, and John Krasinski,

1417
00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:40.310
where we were pitching different ideas.

1418
00:58:40.310 --> 00:58:47.230
And this idea that somehow Jim and Pam would run into Roy and he would have bettered himself,

1419
00:58:47.470 --> 00:58:53.470
like that he had grown in his time away from Pam, this was really important because it was

1420
00:58:53.470 --> 00:58:59.650
supposed to, of course, further highlight how the same Jim and Pam are, how like growth seems

1421
00:58:59.650 --> 00:59:00.590
to have eluded them.

1422
00:59:00.890 --> 00:59:03.550
Not only that, but how they're disconnected.

1423
00:59:04.010 --> 00:59:06.610
Roy and Laura seem very connected.

1424
00:59:06.910 --> 00:59:07.290
Yes.

1425
00:59:07.290 --> 00:59:14.410
I do want to point out one thing in the car, which I find curious at 9 minutes and 24 seconds,

1426
00:59:14.650 --> 00:59:16.710
you can see Cece's car seat in the car.

1427
00:59:17.070 --> 00:59:17.790
Makes sense.

1428
00:59:17.950 --> 00:59:20.630
These things live in a car, but also Phillip's pumpkin seat.

1429
00:59:21.190 --> 00:59:23.390
Usually you leave the pumpkin seat at home, right?

1430
00:59:23.410 --> 00:59:24.750
It just clicks into the base.

1431
00:59:24.990 --> 00:59:25.990
That's how we did it.

1432
00:59:26.050 --> 00:59:29.130
We had a base in each car, but the pumpkin seat goes inside.

1433
00:59:29.130 --> 00:59:35.550
The carrier, the seat, yeah, stays with the child because there's probably a base in Pam's mom's car.

1434
00:59:35.710 --> 00:59:36.170
Exactly.

1435
00:59:36.510 --> 00:59:36.710
Yeah.

1436
00:59:36.950 --> 00:59:37.710
That's a good catch.

1437
00:59:37.850 --> 00:59:42.890
I'll tell you what, Phillip's not going anywhere today because his pumpkin seat is in Jim and Pam's car.

1438
00:59:42.910 --> 00:59:43.150
That's right.

1439
00:59:43.590 --> 00:59:48.550
Well, you know, this car scene, Pam is going to ask Jim to tell her something she doesn't know about him.

1440
00:59:48.830 --> 00:59:50.070
There was more to this moment.

1441
00:59:50.070 --> 00:59:54.250
It's in Deleted Scenes, and I want you to hear how this conversation ended.

1442
00:59:55.390 --> 00:59:57.610
For the record, I like knowing everything about you.

1443
00:59:58.330 --> 00:59:59.150
It's nice.

1444
00:59:59.350 --> 00:59:59.970
Totally agree.

1445
01:00:00.390 --> 01:00:02.070
We know each other inside and out.

1446
01:00:02.570 --> 01:00:03.550
No surprises.

1447
01:00:12.910 --> 01:00:13.430
Yeah.

1448
01:00:13.690 --> 01:00:20.010
She says no surprises, and then his phone starts to buzz, and he doesn't answer it.

1449
01:00:20.070 --> 01:00:20.470
He clicks.

1450
01:00:20.610 --> 01:00:22.450
You know how you can kind of like make it stop buzzing?

1451
01:00:22.970 --> 01:00:25.130
And then they just ride in silence.

1452
01:00:26.470 --> 01:00:28.150
The moment was heavy.

1453
01:00:28.150 --> 01:00:29.070
It was thick.

1454
01:00:29.870 --> 01:00:32.090
I've been re-watching Breaking Bad.

1455
01:00:32.530 --> 01:00:33.270
You have?

1456
01:00:33.470 --> 01:00:34.290
From the beginning.

1457
01:00:34.530 --> 01:00:34.770
Okay.

1458
01:00:35.050 --> 01:00:39.710
I had this theory that Breaking Bad is the best pilot ever made.

1459
01:00:39.890 --> 01:00:40.210
Mm-hmm.

1460
01:00:40.430 --> 01:00:42.490
And, you know, I got sick a couple weeks ago.

1461
01:00:42.730 --> 01:00:46.750
I was laid up for like two weeks, and I was like, what am I going to watch?

1462
01:00:46.910 --> 01:00:53.130
I spent two days watching Law & Order reruns, and I was like, I think I need something with like more of an arc.

1463
01:00:53.290 --> 01:00:54.970
You needed a break from gung gung.

1464
01:00:55.230 --> 01:00:55.410
Yeah.

1465
01:00:56.050 --> 01:00:57.430
So I was like, I'm going to see.

1466
01:00:57.590 --> 01:00:59.550
Is Breaking Bad the best pilot ever made?

1467
01:01:00.090 --> 01:01:00.910
It is.

1468
01:01:01.310 --> 01:01:02.210
It's incredible.

1469
01:01:02.770 --> 01:01:03.850
Then I got hooked.

1470
01:01:04.050 --> 01:01:06.010
Now I'm all the way almost at the end of season three.

1471
01:01:06.190 --> 01:01:11.390
But, you know, a big plot line is Walter White and his burner phone.

1472
01:01:11.390 --> 01:01:11.830
Yeah.

1473
01:01:12.090 --> 01:01:13.670
And it's always buzzing.

1474
01:01:13.890 --> 01:01:14.230
Yeah.

1475
01:01:14.510 --> 01:01:16.030
In those early seasons.

1476
01:01:16.030 --> 01:01:18.750
And his wife is like, what's going on?

1477
01:01:18.830 --> 01:01:20.590
What's with the second cell phone?

1478
01:01:20.730 --> 01:01:20.990
Yeah.

1479
01:01:21.150 --> 01:01:22.250
The second cell phone.

1480
01:01:22.450 --> 01:01:26.210
Are the calls you're not taking in your partner's presence?

1481
01:01:26.390 --> 01:01:27.490
Says it all.

1482
01:01:27.490 --> 01:01:28.670
That says it all.

1483
01:01:28.910 --> 01:01:34.330
If the phone is ringing and they purposely don't answer it, who's on the other end of that call?

1484
01:01:34.330 --> 01:01:35.090
That's right.

1485
01:01:35.210 --> 01:01:38.290
You're either having an affair or you're making meth.

1486
01:01:38.490 --> 01:01:42.130
Or you've started a business with your friend and you're not telling your wife.

1487
01:01:42.290 --> 01:01:43.410
Those are the three things.

1488
01:01:45.560 --> 01:01:51.420
Back at Dunder Mifflin in the kitchen, Jim and Pam are still continuing this like, tell me something I don't know about you.

1489
01:01:51.420 --> 01:01:55.720
And then Jim's like, oh, remember the story about how my brothers tricked me into thinking they won the lottery?

1490
01:01:55.900 --> 01:01:57.480
But Pam already knew that one.

1491
01:01:57.660 --> 01:02:01.900
And then Angela brags that the senator is always surprising her.

1492
01:02:04.300 --> 01:02:07.880
I thought Oscar did such a great job in that moment.

1493
01:02:07.880 --> 01:02:11.120
I had to mimic it because I was like, that was so good.

1494
01:02:11.340 --> 01:02:14.840
I thought it was so good how he just kept choking down his coffee.

1495
01:02:14.860 --> 01:02:18.320
It was such good acting.

1496
01:02:18.380 --> 01:02:19.580
I loved it.

1497
01:02:19.580 --> 01:02:24.720
Have you ever played this game with Josh where you say, like, tell me something I don't know about you?

1498
01:02:24.900 --> 01:02:26.020
Have you ever played this game?

1499
01:02:26.440 --> 01:02:28.420
No, because Josh doesn't want to know.

1500
01:02:28.840 --> 01:02:32.120
He doesn't want to know things he doesn't already know about you?

1501
01:02:32.220 --> 01:02:32.580
Yes.

1502
01:02:32.780 --> 01:02:33.820
This is very funny.

1503
01:02:34.080 --> 01:02:38.160
Yeah, he's just like, I don't need to know anything about past relationships.

1504
01:02:38.260 --> 01:02:41.500
I just need to live in the here and now with you.

1505
01:02:42.100 --> 01:02:44.060
Lee and I like to play this game sometimes.

1506
01:02:44.060 --> 01:02:47.480
If we have a date night, we'll be like, okay, we're not allowed to talk about the kids.

1507
01:02:47.480 --> 01:02:53.000
We're not allowed to talk about school emails or any house improvements or any chores.

1508
01:02:53.280 --> 01:02:53.920
No scheduling.

1509
01:02:54.260 --> 01:02:54.960
Let's be adults together.

1510
01:02:55.380 --> 01:02:58.540
So I'll say, tell me a story I've never heard before.

1511
01:02:58.780 --> 01:02:59.060
Yeah.

1512
01:02:59.520 --> 01:03:03.600
And it often ends up being something rather interesting.

1513
01:03:03.600 --> 01:03:05.380
I have to say, it's a fun little game.

1514
01:03:05.660 --> 01:03:07.600
It's not working for Jim and Pam, though.

1515
01:03:08.840 --> 01:03:11.180
Well, Nellie is going to approach Dwight now.

1516
01:03:11.440 --> 01:03:16.340
Nellie says, look, if you support the Taliban abroad, you have to be willing to live by their

1517
01:03:16.340 --> 01:03:16.900
rules here.

1518
01:03:17.460 --> 01:03:22.160
And she wants that in writing and she hands him a contract, which he signs willingly.

1519
01:03:22.860 --> 01:03:27.380
Dwight then has a talking head where he says, he feared Nellie had a plan, but the ridiculous

1520
01:03:27.380 --> 01:03:28.920
font tells him otherwise.

1521
01:03:29.440 --> 01:03:35.340
Then it cuts to Nellie's talking head and she says, when you use a ridiculous font, no one

1522
01:03:35.340 --> 01:03:36.400
thinks you have a plan.

1523
01:03:36.620 --> 01:03:37.000
Mm-hmm.

1524
01:03:37.200 --> 01:03:39.420
Well, this made me curious about fonts.

1525
01:03:39.720 --> 01:03:40.320
Oh.

1526
01:03:40.560 --> 01:03:46.680
I went online and according to 99designs.com, here's what makes a good font.

1527
01:03:47.060 --> 01:03:48.300
Oh, okay.

1528
01:03:49.100 --> 01:03:50.080
Even kerning.

1529
01:03:50.660 --> 01:03:51.500
What's a kern?

1530
01:03:51.800 --> 01:03:52.360
Exactly.

1531
01:03:52.360 --> 01:03:53.140
What is kerning?

1532
01:03:53.460 --> 01:03:56.160
Kerning is the space between two characters.

1533
01:03:56.400 --> 01:04:01.180
Too little space and the font is unreadable because the letters are smushed together.

1534
01:04:01.180 --> 01:04:05.860
Too much space and it's hard to tell whether the space is meant to separate letters or

1535
01:04:05.860 --> 01:04:06.260
words.

1536
01:04:06.520 --> 01:04:07.740
Oh, that's the kern.

1537
01:04:08.080 --> 01:04:09.100
Even kerning.

1538
01:04:09.360 --> 01:04:11.760
The kern is the space between letters.

1539
01:04:12.420 --> 01:04:14.100
Oh, I want to use that.

1540
01:04:14.280 --> 01:04:18.840
I want to like look at someone's handwriting and say, oh, you have beautiful kerning.

1541
01:04:19.320 --> 01:04:19.820
Yes.

1542
01:04:20.460 --> 01:04:22.980
The next one is consistency.

1543
01:04:23.380 --> 01:04:28.880
For example, Futura and Helvecchia are two examples of fonts that are easy to read because

1544
01:04:28.880 --> 01:04:32.580
of their kerning and the consistency of the letters.

1545
01:04:33.120 --> 01:04:33.540
Okay.

1546
01:04:33.740 --> 01:04:38.800
And if you're wondering what consistency means, it means like if you have a font for the letter

1547
01:04:38.800 --> 01:04:44.920
A and that has serifs, you expect the letter B to have serifs too and so on from there.

1548
01:04:45.400 --> 01:04:51.380
If you're wondering what a serif is, a serif is the mark or line that can sometimes appear

1549
01:04:51.380 --> 01:04:53.700
at the end of a character's stroke.

1550
01:04:53.980 --> 01:04:56.720
Ah, I think I'm picturing a serif.

1551
01:04:56.900 --> 01:04:57.240
Okay.

1552
01:04:57.240 --> 01:04:59.160
Then you want balance.

1553
01:04:59.340 --> 01:05:02.380
You want a blend of thick and thin, heavy and light.

1554
01:05:02.580 --> 01:05:04.940
That's another component of a good font.

1555
01:05:05.200 --> 01:05:07.940
And lastly, it must be legible.

1556
01:05:08.260 --> 01:05:09.720
I have a question for you.

1557
01:05:09.840 --> 01:05:10.080
Yes.

1558
01:05:10.340 --> 01:05:12.160
Do you have a favorite font?

1559
01:05:12.460 --> 01:05:16.580
I like the sans serif typefaces, you know?

1560
01:05:16.760 --> 01:05:19.300
I think the one I use the most is maybe Verdana.

1561
01:05:20.120 --> 01:05:20.400
Mm.

1562
01:05:20.620 --> 01:05:21.000
Mm-hmm.

1563
01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:22.100
What about you?

1564
01:05:22.400 --> 01:05:25.660
Well, for a very long time, I was a Times New Roman.

1565
01:05:25.660 --> 01:05:28.300
That is on the list of worst fonts.

1566
01:05:28.600 --> 01:05:30.340
I have moved to Calibri.

1567
01:05:31.040 --> 01:05:31.920
Calibri's good.

1568
01:05:32.340 --> 01:05:32.920
Another one—

1569
01:05:32.920 --> 01:05:35.020
That's what my document is printed in right now.

1570
01:05:35.160 --> 01:05:36.960
It's printed in Calibri 18.

1571
01:05:37.360 --> 01:05:38.740
I also like Calibri too.

1572
01:05:38.840 --> 01:05:41.120
That is also a sans serif font.

1573
01:05:41.300 --> 01:05:42.160
It is?

1574
01:05:42.260 --> 01:05:42.620
Yes.

1575
01:05:42.620 --> 01:05:43.240
Oh.

1576
01:05:44.020 --> 01:05:46.760
What is—sans means without in French.

1577
01:05:46.760 --> 01:05:47.040
That's right.

1578
01:05:47.080 --> 01:05:48.200
So what is serif?

1579
01:05:48.300 --> 01:05:48.940
What is it without?

1580
01:05:49.340 --> 01:05:53.100
A theme with sans serif typefaces are they're very clean and minimal.

1581
01:05:53.420 --> 01:05:53.720
Mm-hmm.

1582
01:05:53.800 --> 01:05:55.180
They look a little bit more modern.

1583
01:05:55.500 --> 01:05:59.100
If a font has a serif, it has like the finishing of a stroke.

1584
01:05:59.380 --> 01:06:02.880
You know, what's a font that uses serifs is Times New Roman.

1585
01:06:03.420 --> 01:06:03.940
Oh.

1586
01:06:04.280 --> 01:06:04.520
Yeah.

1587
01:06:04.780 --> 01:06:05.220
Okay.

1588
01:06:05.540 --> 01:06:08.800
Also, this website said Garamond is a good one.

1589
01:06:08.800 --> 01:06:11.360
Oh, I've used it before, but—

1590
01:06:11.360 --> 01:06:18.300
And according to several websites—I mean, this one made the list on many websites—one

1591
01:06:18.300 --> 01:06:21.320
of the most horrible fonts is Jokerman.

1592
01:06:21.800 --> 01:06:23.600
I have to look at it.

1593
01:06:23.600 --> 01:06:24.600
I'm so sorry.

1594
01:06:24.800 --> 01:06:26.840
I can put it in stories too.

1595
01:06:27.180 --> 01:06:29.220
I need to see it.

1596
01:06:30.500 --> 01:06:31.200
Oh!

1597
01:06:31.580 --> 01:06:32.180
Oh!

1598
01:06:32.420 --> 01:06:33.220
That's horrible!

1599
01:06:33.540 --> 01:06:35.820
It looks like—oh!

1600
01:06:36.840 --> 01:06:38.800
This looks like what Nellie used.

1601
01:06:40.320 --> 01:06:41.260
It's awful.

1602
01:06:41.460 --> 01:06:44.020
It looks like the letters have a disease.

1603
01:06:44.200 --> 01:06:45.140
It's so busy.

1604
01:06:45.140 --> 01:06:45.190
Doesn't it?

1605
01:06:45.190 --> 01:06:45.860
It's so busy.

1606
01:06:46.100 --> 01:06:46.740
It's so busy.

1607
01:06:47.180 --> 01:06:47.680
Yeah.

1608
01:06:48.100 --> 01:06:52.120
It looks like a plant, the Venus flytrap.

1609
01:06:52.700 --> 01:06:58.240
If you took a Venus flytrap and turned it into letters, that's what this looks like to me.

1610
01:06:58.420 --> 01:06:58.800
Yes.

1611
01:06:59.040 --> 01:06:59.260
Right?

1612
01:06:59.520 --> 01:07:00.980
Well, Jokerman is not popular.

1613
01:07:00.980 --> 01:07:05.640
I'm so sorry to the person who invented the Jokerman font.

1614
01:07:06.020 --> 01:07:06.380
Mm-hmm.

1615
01:07:06.640 --> 01:07:06.920
Sorry, sorry.

1616
01:07:06.920 --> 01:07:10.500
You tried hard, but I think you wanted to edit.

1617
01:07:10.740 --> 01:07:11.600
You did too much.

1618
01:07:12.020 --> 01:07:12.260
Yeah.

1619
01:07:12.520 --> 01:07:13.360
Take one thing away.

1620
01:07:13.480 --> 01:07:14.020
Or three.

1621
01:07:14.380 --> 01:07:15.220
Three things away.

1622
01:07:16.020 --> 01:07:17.460
Angela, that was all very interesting.

1623
01:07:17.720 --> 01:07:24.060
You know, I found it so interesting, just the science behind how we relate to the written

1624
01:07:24.060 --> 01:07:24.900
characters.

1625
01:07:25.320 --> 01:07:25.640
Mm-hmm.

1626
01:07:25.860 --> 01:07:32.160
The door that's opening for me now is who invents fonts and how do you get them, like,

1627
01:07:32.300 --> 01:07:33.100
widely used?

1628
01:07:33.220 --> 01:07:34.280
Who's doing it?

1629
01:07:34.480 --> 01:07:34.700
Yeah.

1630
01:07:34.780 --> 01:07:36.060
What is the font industry?

1631
01:07:36.320 --> 01:07:36.600
Yeah.

1632
01:07:36.780 --> 01:07:37.960
How do you get paid for that?

1633
01:07:38.300 --> 01:07:41.080
Who comes up with this list about even kerning?

1634
01:07:41.660 --> 01:07:42.100
Yeah.

1635
01:07:42.320 --> 01:07:43.960
That's going to keep me up at night now.

1636
01:07:44.180 --> 01:07:44.620
Sorry.

1637
01:07:45.000 --> 01:07:45.640
It's all right.

1638
01:07:46.960 --> 01:07:47.740
Let's see.

1639
01:07:47.920 --> 01:07:52.920
Next up, Andy is going to get word of this newscaster audition, and he has some strong

1640
01:07:52.920 --> 01:07:53.540
feelings about it.

1641
01:07:53.580 --> 01:07:57.940
He is going to storm into the annex and confront Clark.

1642
01:07:57.940 --> 01:08:03.140
He's going to say, what is this that I hear about you wanting to film Aaron in various

1643
01:08:03.140 --> 01:08:04.920
outfits in your apartment?

1644
01:08:05.260 --> 01:08:08.880
My big question is, what specific outfits does she need?

1645
01:08:09.040 --> 01:08:09.900
Oh, Andy.

1646
01:08:10.140 --> 01:08:11.720
He buys into it, too.

1647
01:08:12.020 --> 01:08:12.940
What an idiot.

1648
01:08:14.820 --> 01:08:19.980
Also, at 13 minutes and 23 seconds, this has probably been there the whole episode, but

1649
01:08:19.980 --> 01:08:24.040
it's when I finally noticed that Andy has a grappling hook on his pants.

1650
01:08:24.660 --> 01:08:28.240
You know the thing that, like, if you're climbing a mountain and you have to put the

1651
01:08:28.240 --> 01:08:28.460
thing?

1652
01:08:28.800 --> 01:08:33.880
He's got that hanging off his pants, and also, he started wearing these puffy vest

1653
01:08:33.880 --> 01:08:34.300
coats.

1654
01:08:34.920 --> 01:08:37.340
This is all from his outward-bound experience, I guess.

1655
01:08:37.800 --> 01:08:37.880
Yes.

1656
01:08:38.800 --> 01:08:40.500
He's ready to climb if needed.

1657
01:08:40.720 --> 01:08:40.900
Yep.

1658
01:08:41.180 --> 01:08:46.140
I also wanted to point out in this scene at 13 minutes and 32 seconds, Pete is eating a

1659
01:08:46.140 --> 01:08:48.580
container of baby carrots, just like Jim always does.

1660
01:08:48.860 --> 01:08:50.400
Maybe he is Jim Jr.

1661
01:08:51.300 --> 01:08:54.000
Well, in the kitchen, a few folks have gathered around.

1662
01:08:54.300 --> 01:08:57.240
Jim and Pam are basically playing the newlywed game.

1663
01:08:57.440 --> 01:09:00.360
Kevin wants a lot of personal questions, and Angela's like, no.

1664
01:09:01.100 --> 01:09:03.660
Toby knows all of Pam's answers.

1665
01:09:04.400 --> 01:09:07.500
Pam's first celebrity crush, John Stamos.

1666
01:09:07.760 --> 01:09:11.460
And then it moved quickly to Johnny Depp, which Toby also knew.

1667
01:09:11.860 --> 01:09:14.780
And then he says, sorry, I was having a separate conversation with Kevin.

1668
01:09:15.360 --> 01:09:19.400
And then Kevin at the end says, what's the craziest place you ever made whoopee?

1669
01:09:19.460 --> 01:09:20.760
He brings this question back.

1670
01:09:20.900 --> 01:09:22.080
This is his question.

1671
01:09:22.300 --> 01:09:22.480
Yeah.

1672
01:09:22.820 --> 01:09:25.600
And Angela says, Kevin, stop it with that question.

1673
01:09:26.400 --> 01:09:28.600
That was improvised, that last couplet.

1674
01:09:29.100 --> 01:09:30.800
Kevin isn't supposed to bring it back.

1675
01:09:31.060 --> 01:09:32.480
Angela isn't supposed to say anything.

1676
01:09:32.780 --> 01:09:33.980
But we just kept going.

1677
01:09:34.100 --> 01:09:35.780
They let the cameras roll, and we just kept going.

1678
01:09:36.120 --> 01:09:40.440
When I was watching this scene, it made me remember that when I was younger, before I

1679
01:09:40.440 --> 01:09:41.320
ever moved to L.A.

1680
01:09:41.700 --> 01:09:45.340
growing up, I had two really big celebrity crushes.

1681
01:09:45.540 --> 01:09:45.980
Okay.

1682
01:09:46.120 --> 01:09:50.060
Like posters on my wall, buying the Teen Beat magazines.

1683
01:09:50.440 --> 01:09:50.840
Yeah.

1684
01:09:51.240 --> 01:09:51.720
Huge.

1685
01:09:52.100 --> 01:09:52.440
Huge.

1686
01:09:52.580 --> 01:09:55.920
I never had a celebrity poster on my wall.

1687
01:09:56.060 --> 01:09:56.580
Really?

1688
01:09:56.900 --> 01:09:57.120
Never.

1689
01:09:57.500 --> 01:09:57.740
Okay.

1690
01:09:57.740 --> 01:10:05.980
I did, and when I got to L.A., early in my acting career, I got cast in movies with both

1691
01:10:05.980 --> 01:10:07.520
of my celebrity crushes.

1692
01:10:07.720 --> 01:10:08.940
Can you share who they are?

1693
01:10:09.020 --> 01:10:10.320
I will not share who they are.

1694
01:10:10.320 --> 01:10:10.760
What?

1695
01:10:10.960 --> 01:10:13.740
You got to put that out there and then not tell us?

1696
01:10:13.820 --> 01:10:22.880
Because when I met them in real life, I was not disappointed, but I was in no way attracted

1697
01:10:22.880 --> 01:10:24.500
to them at all.

1698
01:10:24.500 --> 01:10:26.660
Like, I couldn't believe it.

1699
01:10:26.800 --> 01:10:28.960
I was like, how is this possible?

1700
01:10:29.280 --> 01:10:30.600
Like, nothing.

1701
01:10:31.060 --> 01:10:32.160
Like, dead inside.

1702
01:10:33.100 --> 01:10:34.840
Didn't find them hot.

1703
01:10:35.180 --> 01:10:36.820
Didn't get any butterflies.

1704
01:10:37.780 --> 01:10:39.300
Couldn't have been less interested.

1705
01:10:39.500 --> 01:10:41.040
I found it so fascinating.

1706
01:10:41.180 --> 01:10:42.260
It happened twice.

1707
01:10:42.660 --> 01:10:43.280
Wow.

1708
01:10:43.580 --> 01:10:44.020
Twice.

1709
01:10:44.460 --> 01:10:50.200
It wasn't that they were any less good-looking or whatever, but it was just nothing.

1710
01:10:50.560 --> 01:10:51.040
Huh.

1711
01:10:51.040 --> 01:10:56.360
I mean, I guess my only real true celebrity crush has been Keanu Reeves.

1712
01:10:57.120 --> 01:11:01.580
He's never been on a poster in my room, but he is on a coffee mug I have.

1713
01:11:02.680 --> 01:11:04.500
But you've never met him in real life.

1714
01:11:04.680 --> 01:11:09.000
No, but remember I had that moment where my friend Wyatt said that Keanu was on a motorcycle

1715
01:11:09.000 --> 01:11:10.960
and checked out my ass.

1716
01:11:11.120 --> 01:11:11.620
That's right.

1717
01:11:11.640 --> 01:11:15.600
And I'm going to hold on to that forever, whether it was really Keanu or not.

1718
01:11:16.120 --> 01:11:18.440
Someone on a motorcycle checked out your tush.

1719
01:11:21.120 --> 01:11:23.820
And that was 20 years ago, but I'm going to hold on to it.

1720
01:11:24.000 --> 01:11:25.000
Hold on to it.

1721
01:11:25.660 --> 01:11:29.520
Even if it was just a stranger on a motorcycle, you hold that dear.

1722
01:11:29.520 --> 01:11:31.120
Somebody took a double take.

1723
01:11:31.820 --> 01:11:38.860
Well, during this scene, Jim is going to get another phone call, and he's going to excuse himself.

1724
01:11:39.640 --> 01:11:39.940
Yeah.

1725
01:11:40.760 --> 01:11:41.240
Wow.

1726
01:11:43.020 --> 01:11:45.600
And then he has a talking head where he says, you know what?

1727
01:11:45.660 --> 01:11:47.400
I'm not going to tell her until it's real.

1728
01:11:47.980 --> 01:11:53.220
And Pam has a talking head where she says, maybe there is something I don't know about, Jim.

1729
01:11:53.840 --> 01:12:00.240
I think this is a good time to talk about our friend Miles McNutt, because he had a lot of thoughts on this storyline.

1730
01:12:00.460 --> 01:12:02.320
Oh, we haven't heard from Miles in a bit.

1731
01:12:02.320 --> 01:12:03.160
I can't wait.

1732
01:12:03.160 --> 01:12:05.420
I live for our Miles McNutt reviews.

1733
01:12:05.420 --> 01:12:14.760
Well, Miles actually stopped writing reviews for The Office at the end of Season 8, but the new reviewer was out this week, the week of Roy's wedding.

1734
01:12:15.000 --> 01:12:16.660
So Miles wrote this review.

1735
01:12:16.660 --> 01:12:19.460
This was his final review of The Office.

1736
01:12:19.880 --> 01:12:29.520
He gave this episode a B, and he reviewed this episode, but he also kind of gave some thoughts about the new direction of Season 9, which he was liking very much.

1737
01:12:29.680 --> 01:12:32.040
For example, he loves Jerk Andy.

1738
01:12:32.640 --> 01:12:33.180
Okay.

1739
01:12:33.180 --> 01:12:34.420
He liked that change.

1740
01:12:34.560 --> 01:12:39.100
He likes the addition of Clark and Pete, and he loved the Jim-Pam storyline.

1741
01:12:39.620 --> 01:12:41.200
Here's what he said, quote,

1742
01:12:41.700 --> 01:12:49.440
It's the couple's strongest storyline in a long time, and it's also giving the show the kind of arc structure that it lacked last season.

1743
01:12:49.660 --> 01:12:59.100
Heck, it's even retroactively giving last season meaning, with Jim and Pam's boring storylines now becoming a period within their larger arc,

1744
01:12:59.100 --> 01:13:07.280
which was underserved and often outright ignored in Season 8, which I actually thought was such a good point.

1745
01:13:07.460 --> 01:13:19.260
We barely checked in on Jim and Pam's relationship in Season 8, and now this idea that their relationship was too boring to check in on is becoming a major story point.

1746
01:13:19.260 --> 01:13:21.980
So genius of Greg to think of this.

1747
01:13:21.980 --> 01:13:23.420
He went on to say,

1748
01:13:56.920 --> 01:13:57.360
Wow.

1749
01:13:58.360 --> 01:14:07.740
I think that I had to read that because I'm really, really proud because John and I got to be a part of crafting this, and Greg really listened to us,

1750
01:14:07.800 --> 01:14:12.440
and we wanted all the things that he just said to land on the audience, so I was so happy.

1751
01:14:12.440 --> 01:14:14.940
Also, Miles is such a good writer.

1752
01:14:15.120 --> 01:14:15.960
Can we just say that?

1753
01:14:16.020 --> 01:14:16.200
I know.

1754
01:14:16.200 --> 01:14:18.940
Like, everything he wrote there, I was like, yes!

1755
01:14:19.280 --> 01:14:19.900
I know.

1756
01:14:20.080 --> 01:14:22.780
Kind of opened up to how I'm looking at the season now.

1757
01:14:23.080 --> 01:14:24.800
I felt so seen when I read it.

1758
01:14:25.100 --> 01:14:28.160
And then also, Miles signed off in this way.

1759
01:14:28.180 --> 01:14:29.000
He said,

1760
01:14:29.180 --> 01:14:35.220
An earnest thank you to everyone who has read or commented on my review of the show over the past two years.

1761
01:14:35.320 --> 01:14:41.680
It's been incredibly rewarding and enjoyable to go head-to-head about a show that people connect with so personally

1762
01:14:41.680 --> 01:14:43.520
and have strong opinions about.

1763
01:14:43.720 --> 01:14:48.680
And I'll admit that I'll likely be dropping in and out of the comments as the season goes on.

1764
01:14:49.180 --> 01:14:52.440
However, I do so not as a critic but as a viewer.

1765
01:14:52.580 --> 01:14:58.560
So do feel free to insult my person as you would any other commenter with whom you strongly disagree.

1766
01:14:58.980 --> 01:15:00.200
It's only fair.

1767
01:15:01.360 --> 01:15:04.100
Aw, Miles, we're going to miss your reviews.

1768
01:15:04.420 --> 01:15:07.860
Yes, Miles, you have made this rewatch so enjoyable.

1769
01:15:08.000 --> 01:15:11.420
I've loved your insight and your thoughts.

1770
01:15:12.020 --> 01:15:18.340
I'm sad that we won't have any of your reviews for Dwight and Angela's rekindling.

1771
01:15:18.580 --> 01:15:22.520
And I would have loved to have seen your reviews on the Senator and Oscar.

1772
01:15:22.940 --> 01:15:28.060
Yes, Angela, that storyline is not in this episode and he didn't comment on it.

